TY  - JOUR
ID  - 43001258
TI  - The hypertension-NVU-cognition Axis: Mechanisms and interventions.
AU  - Tao Zhengde
JO  - Aging brain
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1016/j.nbas.2026.100174
AB  - Midlife hypertension is a major modifiable risk factor for Alzheimer's disease, but the specific molecular pathways through which it contributes to cognitive decline-pathways that are partially independent of, and interact with, amyloid-β pathology-are not fully elucidated. This review synthesizes and elaborates the "Hypertension-NVU-Cognition" axis, building on the established cerebrovascular biology, neurovascular unit, cerebral small vessel disease (CSVD), and vascular cognitive impairment (VCID) literature, within which this axis is positioned as an integrative, complementary framework rather than a wholly novel or independent pathway. Sustained hypertension damages the neurovascular unit (NVU) through hemodynamic stress, blood-brain barrier (BBB) disruption (via tight junction and pericyte loss), and neuroinflammation (pro-inflammatory microglial activation and reactive astrogliosis). This NVU dysfunction directly impairs cognition via glutamate excitotoxicity, cholinergic axonal ischemia, and reduced Aβ clearance. Critically, midlife blood pressure trajectories are stronger predictors of late-life cognition than late-life levels. NVU injury frequently coexists with amyloid pathology, yet emerging evidence indicates it also contributes to cognitive decline in individuals with low amyloid burden, highlighting its complementary and potentially synergistic role alongside classical AD mechanisms. This axis highlights candidate molecular targets and biomarkers that merit further investigation for early intervention. Promising biomarkers include plasma sPDGFRβ (pericyte injury) and DCE-MRI Ktrans (BBB leakage). Promising future research directions include standardizing NVU biomarkers and conducting targeted trials that combine intensive blood pressure control with investigational NVU-protective agents (e.g., GLP-1 receptor agonists) in at-risk midlife individuals; these approaches are not yet clinically actionable and require validation in adequately powered randomized controlled trials. Understanding this axis is crucial for developing effective preventive therapies against vascular cognitive impairment.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43001258/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 43000472
TI  - Hemodynamic effects of semaglutide in individuals with heart failure with preserved ejection fraction and obesity: a placebo-controlled randomized clinical trial.
AU  - Borlaug Barry A
AU  - Jensen Michael D
AU  - Packer Milton
AU  - Reddy Yogesh N V
AU  - Harada Tomonari
AU  - Ghanbari Fahime
AU  - Johnson Patrick W
AU  - Carter Rickey E
JO  - Nature medicine
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1038/s41591-026-04762-3
AB  - The glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonist semaglutide improves symptoms in heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF) through unclear mechanisms. Here, in this double-blind, placebo-controlled trial, we randomly assigned 81 patients (47 women) with HFpEF and obesity to semaglutide (2.4 mg weekly) or placebo for 52 weeks. The primary outcome was the change in invasively measured pulmonary artery wedge pressure (PAWP), a direct measure of cardiac congestion, during low-intensity exercise. Additional mechanistic outcomes included other hemodynamic measures and hemodynamic HFpEF remission (defined as normal PAWP under conditions of both rest and exercise at 52 weeks). At baseline, PAWP was 17.5 ± 4.3 mmHg at rest and 31.7 ± 5.2 mmHg during exercise. After 52 weeks, the mean change in PAWP during exercise was -9.8 (95% confidence interval (CI): -11.3 to -8.2) mmHg with semaglutide and +0.9 (95% CI: -1.3 to 3.0) mmHg with placebo (estimated difference, -10.6 mmHg; 95% CI: -13.3 to -8.0; P < 0.0001). As compared to placebo, semaglutide also decreased resting PAWP (estimated difference, -7.1 mmHg; 95% CI: -9.8 to -4.4; P < 0.0001), and semaglutide-treated patients were more likely to experience hemodynamic remission (53.7% versus 7.4%, odds ratio = 14.1, 95% CI: 3.0-134.6, P < 0.001). The results from this trial indicate that, in patients with HFpEF and obesity, treatment with semaglutide reduced left heart filling pressures during exercise and at rest and was associated with hemodynamic HFpEF remission in over half of treated patients. ClinicalTrials.gov identifier: NCT05371496 .
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43000472/
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
ID  - 43000112
TI  - Effects of semaglutide on cardiac structure and function in heart failure with preserved ejection fraction and obesity: a pre-specified cardiac magnetic resonance substudy of the CAMEO-SEMA trial.
AU  - Ghanbari Fahime
AU  - Reddy Yogesh N V
AU  - Packer Milton
AU  - Ng Arnold
AU  - Carter Rickey
AU  - Jensen Michael D
AU  - Leiner Tim
AU  - Borlaug Barry A
JO  - Nature medicine
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.1038/s41591-026-04787-8
AB  - Glucagon-like peptide-1 receptor agonists improve symptoms in patients with obesity and heart failure with preserved ejection fraction (HFpEF). We recently demonstrated that semaglutide reduces exertional pulmonary artery wedge pressure (PAWP) in obesity-related HFpEF, but potential effects of semaglutide on cardiac structure and function that may underlie these hemodynamic improvements remain unclear. In this prespecified cardiovascular magnetic resonance (CMR) substudy of the randomized CAMEO-SEMA trial, 49 participants (33 semaglutide, 16 placebo, 28 female) underwent CMR at baseline and 52 weeks, within 1 day of invasive exercise hemodynamic assessment. The prespecified primary CMR endpoint was change in left atrial (LA) reservoir strain; other prespecified CMR endpoints included LV filling dynamics, which were assessed by early filling rate indexed to end-diastolic volume (eFR/EDV). Compared with placebo, semaglutide improved LA reservoir strain (+5.7% [95% CI, +0.3 to +9.7]; P=0.037) and eFR/EDV (+0.64 s-1 [95% CI, +0.42 to +1.00]; P<0.0001). Improvements in LA reservoir strain and LV filling dynamics were associated with reductions in exercise PAWP (r=-0.46, P<0.001 and r=-0.65, P<0.0001, respectively). With respect to other prespecified CMR endpoints, semaglutide reduced LV mass, restored more physiologic LV geometry, and improved right ventricular function and biventricular myocardial mechanics. The results of this CMR substudy indicate that in patients with obesity-related HFpEF, semaglutide improves LA function and LV diastolic filling dynamics, identifying improvements in myocardial function that may contribute to semaglutide's improvement of hemodynamics. ClinicalTrials.gov number: NCT05371496.
UR  - https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/43000112/
